  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why \"pure\" gold is labeled as 99.9% (or 999 fineness) rather than 100%:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2c It's Physically Impossible to Achieve 100% Purity\n\nGold that is 100% pure is nearly impossible to achieve. Gold is made up of atoms, and perfect purity would mean a piece of gold has zero non-gold atoms \u2014 but nature has its own plans, and pure gold naturally has some other atoms mingling in there.\n\nThese so-called \"impurities\" actually have a job \u2014 they help stabilize the metal's structure. They are a small amount, but it makes a world of difference to the end product. Take out all the impurities, and the precious metal might just not act like the gold we know and love.\n\n---\n\n### \u2699\ufe0f The Limits of Refining Technology\n\nIt is currently impossible to refine gold to a purity of 100% (1,000 fineness). 999.999 fineness is the purest gold ever produced, and proved too expensive to be a regular occurrence.\n\nGenerally, the closest to 100% pure gold that refiners are able to reach in everyday production is a fineness of 999.9 gold.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcb0 The Cost Factor\n\nRefining metals to remove impurities costs money. There's no benefit in making gold bullion used for monetary or jewelry means any better than 99.99%, as doing so just raises the cost with no benefit.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udccf How Purity is Measured\n\nGold's purity is graded based on the **millesimal fineness scale**, which is used to determine the purity of a precious metal by measuring the number of parts per thousand of pure metal. Typically, anything that is 99.9% gold (999) or more is considered fine gold.\n\nThe purest gold commercially available is 999.99, also called \"five nines\" fine. Just below this is 999.9 gold, also called \"four nines\" fine.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc5 Industry Standards\n\nAnything at or above 999 gold is still considered pure and would fetch the same price when sold. The British Royal Mint, the Royal Canadian Mint, and the Perth Mint all strike their gold coins to a purity of 999.9 gold, making it one of the most commonly seen purities for bullion coins and bars.\n\n---\n\n### In Summary\n\nThe 99.9% label exists because:\n1. **Nature** \u2014 Gold always contains trace amounts of other atoms that are practically impossible to fully eliminate.\n2. **Technology** \u2014 Current refining technology cannot achieve absolute 100% purity.\n3. **Economics** \u2014 Pushing beyond 99.9% purity becomes extremely costly with diminishing returns.\n4. **Convention** \u2014 The industry has standardized 999 (99.9%) as the benchmark for \"pure\" gold, which is universally accepted.",
